## Verdict

Passed. `studio/briefs/W-132.md` revision 1 signs one decree family: the
transport under W-125's Git broker. Four numbered behaviours, eight owned
paths, no new runtime dependency, no CLI surface.

## Measurement before design

Ten measurements were run inside this session's Claude Code bwrap sandbox
before any design, and the commands and outputs are retained in
`~/.bisellium-evidence/W-132-spec/` (`m1-sockets.log`, `m2-bwrap.log`,
`m3-fds.log`, `m4-inner.log`, `m5-git.log`, `m6-misc.log`, `m7-duplex.log`,
`m8-depth.log`, `m9-fifo.log`, `m10-tamper.log`).

The socket `listen()` is the only blocker on the runner's path. Nested
bubblewrap with the runner's exact `--unshare-all` shape exits 0, as does
every namespace alone; `clone --no-local`, in-clone `config`, `commit`,
`bundle create`, `bundle unbundle`, `update-ref` and `fsck --full` all work;
the lease directory, the dependency copy and the cell's loopback bind work.
The host repo's `.git/config` is unwritable, but the runner never writes it.
The unix-socket denial is inherited into the cell as well as the host.

## Why the design is not inherited stdio fds alone

M8 is the finding that shaped the brief. An inherited fd reaches only a child
whose parent explicitly maps it: `direct-mapped: REACHED`,
`node-mapped-then-bash: REACHED`, `node-unmapped-then-bash: UNREACHABLE
EINVAL`. The builder command is an arbitrary agent, and every `git` it runs
is spawned by a process it created without mapping fd 3, so an inherited-fd
channel is severed before `git` ever sees it. A duplex inherited fd does work
through the nested cell (M7) and is admitted as an addition, never as the
only path.

The brief therefore specifies a host-created named-pipe pair inside the
existing read-only `/control` bind: reached by path exactly as the socket
was, proved from a node to shell to node chain with no fd inheritance (M9),
and with the cell unable to unlink, rename, chmod or plant in `/control`
(M10, all EROFS). It is a pipe; it needs no `listen()`. This is a deviation
from the opus title's literal "inherited pipes", taken on the measurement
and recorded here for the Patron's veto.

## Trust map

W-125's map is carried over whole and is the standard the censor judges
against under D-035. Policy stays in `run-builder-host.mjs`: the new
`scripts/git-broker.mjs` is transport only and takes `serve` as an argument,
so `validateGitArgs`, `GIT_LOCKDOWN`, `brokerEnv`, the probe-index branch and
the `/workspace` mask stay in one file with one allowlist. Raw `.git` stays
read-only; `/control` and `/tools` stay read-only; the probe's scratch index
stays out of the cell; export, publication, replay, the producer gate, the
receipt and review admission are untouched. The request pipe is the new
untrusted input and is bounded at the 128 KiB cap the socket path already
used, with malformed frames answered and never passed to `serve`. The
cell-side mutex and request id are named in the brief as correctness, not
security: a builder that breaks them confuses only itself.

## Replayability

Each behaviour's red is assertion-level at a clean committed pre-change
identity, one log per behaviour, selected by a whitespace-free token with
`--grep` and no `^` anchor. Because the producer replay rejects output
matching `not permitted|permission denied|sandbox|ENOENT|command not found`,
the brief forbids a red row from interpolating a child's stderr into an
assertion message, forbids the word `sandbox` in any title or message on a
red, and requires behaviours 3 and 4 to assert a declared module's absence
explicitly rather than let a load error stand in for it. The acceptance
makes a skipped live row a failure, and binds W-132's own receipt to a
`bisellium run` executed from a checkout that already carries the fix,
because the pre-fix runner cannot start inside the cell.

## Two hazards handed to the builder

Both were measured, not predicted. A blocking `openSync(fifo, "r")` on the
event loop deadlocks the runner before it spawns the cell, so both pipes open
`O_RDWR` and are read through a stream. An open pipe handle keeps the event
loop alive, so the channel's `close()` joins teardown and the signal handlers
or the runner never exits and every live row times out.

## Scope

Out of scope is explicit about what must not move: the Git allowlist, the
environment allowlist, clone/export/publication, red semantics, the producer
gate, the receipt schema, the `builder_runtime` marker, review admission,
and any socket including TCP loopback as the control channel. W-132 lands
before W-130 and W-110, which are both blocked on a receipt no runner can
currently mint; W-130 rebases onto it and re-attests its reds by replay as
W-125 revision 5 prescribes.

No implementation, test or evidence is produced by this signing. D-014's
censor supplies the single independent review.
